{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/proposal.md; grep -R -n \\\"ExceptionGroup\\\\|exception group\\\" src/flask tests 2>/dev/null | head -40; grep -R -n \\\"tearing_down.connect\\\\|request_tearing_down\\\\|appcontext_tearing_down\\\" tests 2>/dev/null | head -80\"","aggregated_output":"# Proposal\n\n## Why\n\nAn exception from a teardown callback currently prevents later callbacks and\nthe teardown signal from running, which can leave resources unclosed. Teardown\nmust make a best effort to finish registered cleanup while still reporting its\nfailures.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- Run every request and application-context teardown callback even if one or\n  more callbacks fail.\n- Send the corresponding teardown signal after callback failures as part of the\n  same best-effort cleanup.\n- Raise collected teardown failures only after cleanup and context restoration;\n  use an exception group where the running Python supports it.\n- Add regression tests for callback, signal, error aggregation, and\n  context-restoration behavior.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n\n- `resilient-teardown-cleanup`: Best-effort request and application-context\n  teardown with deferred error reporting.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\n\nNone.\n\n## Impact\n\nChanges `src/flask/app.py` and `src/flask/ctx.py`, with focused coverage in\nthe context teardown tests. Applications may observe grouped teardown errors\non supported Python versions after all cleanup has run.\ntests/test_signals.py:163:def test_appcontext_tearing_down_signal(app, client):\ntests/test_signals.py:174:    flask.appcontext_tearing_down.connect(record_teardown, app)\ntests/test_signals.py:181:        flask.appcontext_tearing_down.disconnect(record_teardown, app)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
