{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"openspec status --change resilient-teardown-cleanup --json && sed -n '1,240p' openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/proposal.md && sed -n '1,260p' openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/specs/resilient-teardown-cleanup/spec.md && sed -n '1,260p' openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/design.md && openspec instructions tasks --change resilient-teardown-cleanup --json\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"changeName\": \"resilient-teardown-cleanup\",\n  \"schemaName\": \"spec-driven\",\n  \"planningHome\": {\n    \"kind\": \"repo\",\n    \"root\": \"/workspace\",\n    \"changesDir\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes\",\n    \"defaultSchema\": \"spec-driven\"\n  },\n  \"changeRoot\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup\",\n  \"artifactPaths\": {\n    \"proposal\": {\n      \"outputPath\": \"proposal.md\",\n      \"resolvedOutputPath\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/proposal.md\",\n      \"existingOutputPaths\": [\n        \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/proposal.md\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"specs\": {\n      \"outputPath\": \"specs/**/*.md\",\n      \"resolvedOutputPath\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/specs/**/*.md\",\n      \"existingOutputPaths\": [\n        \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/specs/resilient-teardown-cleanup/spec.md\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"design\": {\n      \"outputPath\": \"design.md\",\n      \"resolvedOutputPath\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/design.md\",\n      \"existingOutputPaths\": [\n        \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/design.md\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tasks\": {\n      \"outputPath\": \"tasks.md\",\n      \"resolvedOutputPath\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/tasks.md\",\n      \"existingOutputPaths\": []\n    }\n  },\n  \"isPlanningComplete\": false,\n  \"isComplete\": false,\n  \"applyRequires\": [\n    \"tasks\"\n  ],\n  \"nextSteps\": [\n    \"Run openspec instructions tasks --change \\\"resilient-teardown-cleanup\\\" --json before writing that artifact.\"\n  ],\n  \"actionContext\": {\n    \"mode\": \"repo-local\",\n    \"sourceOfTruth\": \"repo\",\n    \"planningArtifacts\": [\n      \"proposal\",\n      \"specs\",\n      \"design\",\n      \"tasks\"\n    ],\n    \"linkedContext\": [],\n    \"allowedEditRoots\": [\n      \"/workspace\"\n    ],\n    \"requiresAffectedAreaSelection\": false,\n    \"constraints\": [\n      \"Repo-local change artifacts and implementation edits are scoped to this project.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"proposal\",\n      \"outputPath\": \"proposal.md\",\n      \"status\": \"done\",\n      \"requires\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"specs\",\n      \"outputPath\": \"specs/**/*.md\",\n      \"status\": \"done\",\n      \"requires\": [\n        \"proposal\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"design\",\n      \"outputPath\": \"design.md\",\n      \"status\": \"done\",\n      \"requires\": [\n        \"proposal\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"tasks\",\n      \"outputPath\": \"tasks.md\",\n      \"status\": \"ready\",\n      \"requires\": [\n        \"specs\",\n        \"design\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"root\": {\n    \"path\": \"/workspace\",\n    \"source\": \"nearest\"\n  }\n}\n# Proposal\n\n## Why\n\nAn exception from a teardown callback currently prevents later callbacks and\nthe teardown signal from running, which can leave resources unclosed. Teardown\nmust make a best effort to finish registered cleanup while still reporting its\nfailures.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- Run every request and application-context teardown callback even if one or\n  more callbacks fail.\n- Send the corresponding teardown signal after callback failures as part of the\n  same best-effort cleanup.\n- Raise collected teardown failures only after cleanup and context restoration;\n  use an exception group where the running Python supports it.\n- Add regression tests for callback, signal, error aggregation, and\n  context-restoration behavior.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n\n- `resilient-teardown-cleanup`: Best-effort request and application-context\n  teardown with deferred error reporting.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\n\nNone.\n\n## Impact\n\nChanges `src/flask/app.py` and `src/flask/ctx.py`, with focused coverage in\nthe context teardown tests. Applications may observe grouped teardown errors\non supported Python versions after all cleanup has run.\n# Spec Delta\n\n## Purpose\n\nEnsure Flask context teardown completes registered cleanup despite individual\ncleanup failures and reports those failures only after context cleanup.\n\n## ADDED Requirements\n\n### Requirement: Teardown completes best-effort cleanup\nDuring request and application-context teardown, Flask MUST invoke every\nregistered teardown callback in its existing order and MUST send the matching\nteardown signal even if preceding teardown callbacks or signal receivers raise.\nAfter all teardown work has been attempted, Flask MUST raise the collected\nteardown failures. On Python versions with exception groups, the raised error\nMUST preserve every collected failure; on older Python versions, Flask MAY\nraise the first collected failure.\n\n#### Scenario: Multiple request teardown callbacks fail\n- **WHEN** more than one request teardown callback raises during context pop\n- **THEN** later callbacks and the request teardown signal are invoked before\n  the collected teardown failures are raised\n\n#### Scenario: Application teardown signal receiver fails\n- **WHEN** an application-context teardown signal receiver raises\n- **THEN** Flask finishes attempting the signal's remaining receivers and\n  raises the collected failure after callbacks and receivers complete\n\n### Requirement: Context state is restored after teardown failure\nFlask MUST restore context-local state before a deferred teardown failure\nleaves a request or application context.\n\n#### Scenario: Context pop reports cleanup failures\n- **WHEN** teardown raises one or more deferred failures while popping a context\n- **THEN** no application or request context remains active after the error is\n  caught\n# Design\n\n## Context\n\n`Flask.do_teardown_request` and `Flask.do_teardown_appcontext` invoke callback\nlists followed by Blinker signals directly. A raised exception interrupts that\nsequence. `AppContext.pop` resets its context variable in a `finally` block,\nbut an application teardown failure can currently prevent later signal work.\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n\n- Centralize collection and deferred raising of teardown failures.\n- Preserve callback ordering and continue across both callbacks and signal\n  receivers.\n- Ensure the context variable reset remains unconditional before deferred\n  errors escape.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n\n- Change normal request dispatch or non-teardown signal error behavior.\n- Change teardown callback registration order or exception arguments.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- Introduce a small shared Flask helper that runs an iterable of cleanup\n  callables, collects `BaseException` instances, and raises after the iterable\n  is exhausted. This avoids duplicating collection rules between request and\n  application-context teardown.\n- Invoke teardown signal receivers through the same resilient mechanism rather\n  than relying on the signal's aggregate send method, because `send` stops at\n  its first receiver error. Preserve signal sender, arguments, and Flask's\n  async wrapper when invoking each receiver.\n- Use `BaseExceptionGroup` when it exists so every failure is retained. Keep a\n  compatibility fallback that raises the first error on older supported Python\n  versions.\n\nAlternatives considered: nesting `try`/`except` around each call in both\nteardown methods duplicates error policy and does not make signal receivers\nresilient; suppressing failures loses teardown diagnostics.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- [Signal receiver ordering is managed by Blinker] → Preserve the order\n  returned by the signal's receiver iterator and cover all receivers in tests.\n- [Teardown can now expose grouped errors] → This is the settled compatibility\n  behavior and occurs only after all cleanup completes.\n\n## Migration Plan\n\nNo deployment or data migration is required. Existing applications retain\ncallback ordering; applications that inspect teardown failures may receive an\nexception group on supported Python versions.\n{\n  \"changeName\": \"resilient-teardown-cleanup\",\n  \"artifactId\": \"tasks\",\n  \"schemaName\": \"spec-driven\",\n  \"changeDir\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup\",\n  \"planningHome\": {\n    \"kind\": \"repo\",\n    \"root\": \"/workspace\",\n    \"changesDir\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes\",\n    \"defaultSchema\": \"spec-driven\"\n  },\n  \"outputPath\": \"tasks.md\",\n  \"resolvedOutputPath\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/tasks.md\",\n  \"existingOutputPaths\": [],\n  \"description\": \"Implementation checklist with trackable tasks\",\n  \"instruction\": \"Create the task list that breaks down the implementation work.\\n\\nBefore writing tasks, check design.md for Open Questions. If any of them\\nwould change what gets built, resolve them with the user first - do not\\nbake an unstated assumption into the task list.\\n\\n**IMPORTANT: Follow the template below exactly.** The apply phase parses\\ncheckbox format to track progress. A box holding only `x` counts as done,\\nupper or lower case and with any spacing, so `- [ x]` is done too. Every\\nother marker, including `- [~]`, `- [-]` and an empty `- []`, reads as\\nunfinished. A line with no checkbox is not tracked at all.\\n\\nGuidelines:\\n- Group related tasks under ## numbered headings\\n- Each task MUST be a checkbox: `- [ ] X.Y Task description`\\n- Tasks should be small enough to complete in one session\\n- Order tasks by dependency (what must be done first?)\\n- Each task MUST state how to verify completion (a test, command,\\n  observable behavior, or delivered artifact). Put the verification in\\n  that task's checkbox description. Use a separate verification task only\\n  when it checks broader integration or system behavior that spans\\n  multiple implementation tasks.\\n\\nExample:\\n```\\n# Tasks\\n\\n## 1. Setup\\n\\n- [ ] 1.1 Create new module structure and verify expected files are present\\n- [ ] 1.2 Add dependencies to package.json and verify package installation succeeds\\n\\n## 2. Core Implementation\\n\\n- [ ] 2.1 Implement data export function and verify the export test passes\\n- [ ] 2.2 Add CSV formatting utilities and verify unit tests cover quoting and delimiters\\n```\\n\\nReference specs for what needs to be built, design for how to build it.\\n\",\n  \"template\": \"# Tasks\\n\\n## 1. <!-- Task Group Name -->\\n\\n- [ ] 1.1 <!-- Task description -->\\n- [ ] 1.2 <!-- Task description -->\\n\\n## 2. <!-- Task Group Name -->\\n\\n- [ ] 2.1 <!-- Task description -->\\n- [ ] 2.2 <!-- Task description -->\\n\",\n  \"dependencies\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"specs\",\n      \"done\": true,\n      \"path\": \"specs/**/*.md\",\n      \"description\": \"Detailed specifications for the change\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"design\",\n      \"done\": true,\n      \"path\": \"design.md\",\n      \"description\": \"Technical design document with implementation details\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"unlocks\": [],\n  \"root\": {\n    \"path\": \"/workspace\",\n    \"source\": \"nearest\"\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
